(; constants used by Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Div, declared with their translated types ;)

(; a constant applied as a function: DFunLike.coe ;)
Polynomial__C__app : (a1 : Elem R) -> Elem (Polynomial R).

(; a constant applied as a function: DFunLike.coe ;)
Polynomial__coeff__app : (a1 : Elem (Polynomial R)) -> (a2 : Elem Nat) -> Elem R.

(; a constant applied as a function: DFunLike.coe ;)
Polynomial__monomial__app : (a1 : Elem Nat) -> (a2 : Elem R) -> Elem (Polynomial R).

(; doc: Two elements of a `Monoid` are `Associated` if one of them is another one multiplied by a unit on the right. ;)
(; //LEAN:Associated ;)
Associated : (M : Set) -> (x1 : Elem M) -> (y : Elem M) -> Prop.

(; doc: A commutative ring is a ring with commutative multiplication. ;)
(; //LEAN:CommRing ;)
CommRing : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A commutative semiring is a semiring with commutative multiplication. ;)
(; //LEAN:CommSemiring ;)
CommSemiring : (R : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: `Eq.refl a : a = a` is reflexivity, the unique constructor of the equality type. See also `rfl`, which is usually used instead. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.refl ;)
Eq__refl : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X a a).

(; doc: Equality is symmetric: if `a = b` then `b = a`.  Because this is in the `Eq` namespace, if you have a variable `h : a = b`, `h.symm` can be used as shorthand for `Eq.symm h` as a proof of `b = a`.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.symm ;)
Eq__symm : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X a b)) -> Proof (Eqt X b a).

(; doc: Equality is transitive: if `a = b` and `b = c` then `a = c`.  Because this is in the `Eq` namespace, if you have variables or expressions `h₁ : a = b` and `h₂ : b = c`, you can use `h₁.trans h₂ : a = c` as shorthand for `Eq.trans h₁ h₂`.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.trans ;)
Eq__trans : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (c : Elem X) -> (h1 : Proof (Eqt X a b)) -> (h2 : Proof (Eqt X b c)) -> Proof (Eqt X a c).

(; doc: A `Field` is a `CommRing` with multiplicative inverses for nonzero elements.  An instance of `Field K` includes maps `ratCast : ℚ → K` and `qsmul : ℚ → K → K`. Those two fields are needed to implement the `DivisionRing K → Algebra ℚ K` instance since we need to control the specific definitions for some special cases of `K` (in particular `K = ℚ` itself). See also note [forgetful inheritance].  If the field has positive characteristic `p`, our division by zero convention forces `ratCast (1 / p) = 1 / 0 = 0`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Field ;)
Field : (K : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Natural numbers less than some upper bound.  In particular, a `Fin n` is a natural number `i` with the constraint that `i < n`. It is the canonical type with `n` elements. ;)
(; //LEAN:Fin ;)
Fin : (n : Elem Nat) -> Set.

(; doc: The number that is strictly less than `n`.  `Fin.val` is a coercion, so any `Fin n` can be used in a position where a `Nat` is expected. ;)
(; //LEAN:Fin.val ;)
Fin__val : (n : Elem Nat) -> (self : Elem (Fin n)) -> Elem Nat.

(; doc: `FiniteMultiplicity a b` indicates that the multiplicity of `a` in `b` is finite. ;)
(; //LEAN:FiniteMultiplicity ;)
FiniteMultiplicity : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> Prop.

(; doc: The finset $[a, b]$ of elements `x` such that `a ≤ x` and `x ≤ b`. Basically `Set.Icc a b` as a finset. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.Icc ;)
Finset__Icc : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> Elem (Finset X).

(; doc: `∑ x ∈ s, f x` is the sum of `f x` as `x` ranges over the elements of the finite set `s`.  When the index type is a `Fintype`, the notation `∑ x, f x`, is a shorthand for `∑ x ∈ Finset.univ, f x`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.sum ;)
Finset__sum : (I : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (s : Elem (Finset I)) -> (f : (Elem I -> M)) -> Elem M.

(; doc: `univ` is the universal finite set of type `Finset α` implied from the assumption `Fintype α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.univ ;)
Finset__univ : (X : Set) -> Elem (Finset X).

(; doc: A function `f : α → β` is called injective if `f x = f y` implies `x = y`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.Injective ;)
Function__Injective : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:Iff.trans ;)
Iff__trans : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (c : Prop) -> (h1 : Proof (iff a b)) -> (h2 : Proof (iff b c)) -> Proof (iff a c).

(; doc: `Irreducible p` states that `p` is non-unit and only factors into units.  We explicitly avoid stating that `p` is non-zero, this would require a semiring. Assuming only a monoid allows us to reuse irreducible for associated elements.  [Wikidata Q2989575](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2989575) ;)
(; //LEAN:Irreducible ;)
Irreducible : (M : Set) -> (p : Elem M) -> Prop.

(; doc: A domain is a nontrivial semiring such that multiplication by a nonzero element is cancellative on both sides. In other words, a nontrivial semiring `R` satisfying `∀ {a b c : R}, a ≠ 0 → a * b = a * c → b = c` and `∀ {a b c : R}, b ≠ 0 → a * b = c * b → a = c`.  This is implemented as a mixin for `Semiring α`. To obtain an integral domain use `[CommRing α] [IsDomain α]`. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsDomain ;)
IsDomain : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: An element `a : M` of a `Monoid` is a unit if it has a two-sided inverse. The actual definition says that `a` is equal to some `u : Mˣ`, where `Mˣ` is a bundled version of `IsUnit`. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsUnit ;)
IsUnit : (M : Set) -> (a : Elem M) -> Prop.

(; doc: Units of a monoid divide any element of the monoid. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsUnit.dvd ;)
IsUnit__dvd : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Monoid X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (u : Elem X) -> (hu : Proof (IsUnit X u)) -> Proof (divide u a).

(; //LEAN:IsUnit.dvd._simp_1 ;)
IsUnit__dvd___simp_1 : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Monoid X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (u : Elem X) -> (hu : Proof (IsUnit X u)) -> Proof (Eqt Prop (divide u a) True).

(; doc: Although `TrailingParserDescr` is an abbreviation for `ParserDescr`, Lean will look at the declared type in order to determine whether to add the parser to the leading or trailing parser table. The determination is done automatically by the `syntax` command. ;)
(; //LEAN:Lean.TrailingParserDescr ;)
Lean__TrailingParserDescr : Set.

(; doc: The membership relation `a ∈ s : Prop` where `a : α`, `s : γ`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `∈` in identifiers is `mem`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Membership.mem ;)
Membership__mem : (X : Set) -> (Z : Set) -> (x : Elem Z) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Prop.

(; doc: Returns the lesser of its two arguments.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `min` in identifiers is `min`.   * The recommended spelling of `⊓` in identifiers is `inf` (`⊓` is the preferred notation for `min` when the type is not linearly ordered.). ;)
(; //LEAN:Min.min ;)
Min__min : (X : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: The canonical homomorphism `Nat → R`. In most use cases, the target type will have a (semi)ring structure, and this homomorphism should be a (semi)ring homomorphism.  `NatCast` and `IntCast` exist to allow different libraries with their own types that can be notated as natural numbers to have consistent `simp` normal forms without needing to create coercion simplification sets that are aware of all combinations. Libraries should make it easy to work with `NatCast` where possible. For instance, in Mathlib there will be such a homomorphism (and thus a `NatCast R` instance) whenever `R` is an additive monoid with a `1`.  The prototypical example is `Int.ofNat`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Nat.cast ;)
Nat__cast : (R : Set) -> (x1 : Elem Nat) -> Elem R.

(; doc: If `p` is a (decidable) predicate on `ℕ` and `hp : ∃ (n : ℕ), p n` is a proof that there exists some natural number satisfying `p`, then `Nat.find hp` is the smallest natural number satisfying `p`. Note that `Nat.find` is protected, meaning that you can't just write `find`, even if the `Nat` namespace is open.  The API for `Nat.find` is:  * `Nat.find_spec` is the proof that `Nat.find hp` satisfies `p`. * `Nat.find_min` is the proof that if `m < Nat.find hp` then `m` does not satisfy `p`. * `Nat.find_min'` is the proof that if `m` does satisfy `p` then `Nat.find hp ≤ m`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Nat.find ;)
Nat__find : (p : (Elem Nat -> Prop)) -> (H : Proof (exists Nat (n => p n))) -> Elem Nat.

(; doc: `-a` computes the negative or opposite of `a`. The meaning of this notation is type-dependent.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `-` in identifiers is `neg` (when used as a unary operator). ;)
(; //LEAN:Neg.neg ;)
Neg__neg : (X : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: Fully universe-polymorphic dependent pairs, in which the second element's type depends on the value of the first element and both types are allowed to be propositions. The type `PSigma β` is typically written `Σ' a : α, β a` or `(a : α) ×' β a`.  In practice, this generality leads to universe level constraints that are difficult to solve, so `PSigma` is rarely used in manually-written code. It is usually only used in automation that constructs pairs of arbitrary types.  To pair a value with a proof that a predicate holds for it, use `Subtype`. To demonstrate that a value exists that satisfies a predicate, use `Exists`. A dependent pair with a proposition as its first component is not typically useful due to proof irrelevance: there's no point in depending on a specific proof because all proofs are equal anyway. ;)
(; //LEAN:PSigma ;)
PSigma : (X : Set) -> (beta : (Elem X -> Set)) -> Set.

(; doc: Constructs a fully universe-polymorphic dependent pair. ;)
(; //LEAN:PSigma.mk ;)
PSigma__mk : (X : Set) -> (beta : (Elem X -> Set)) -> (fst : Elem X) -> (snd : Elem (beta fst)) -> Elem (PSigma X beta).

(; doc: `Polynomial R` is the type of univariate polynomials over `R`, denoted as `R[X]` within the `Polynomial` namespace.  Polynomials should be seen as (semi-)rings with the additional constructor `X`. The embedding from `R` is called `C`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Polynomial ;)
Polynomial : (R : Set) -> Set.

(; //LEAN:Polynomial.C_0 ;)
Polynomial__C_0 : (R : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring R)) -> Proof (Eqt (Polynomial R) (Polynomial__C__app 0) 0).

(; //LEAN:Polynomial.C_inj ;)
Polynomial__C_inj : (R : Set) -> (a : Elem R) -> (b : Elem R) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring R)) -> Proof (iff (Eqt (Polynomial R) (Polynomial__C__app a) (Polynomial__C__app b)) (Eqt R a b)).

(; doc: `IsRoot p x` implies `x` is a root of `p`. The evaluation of `p` at `x` is zero ;)
(; //LEAN:Polynomial.IsRoot ;)
Polynomial__IsRoot : (R : Set) -> (p : Elem (Polynomial R)) -> (a : Elem R) -> Prop.

(; doc: a polynomial is `Monic` if its leading coefficient is 1 ;)
(; //LEAN:Polynomial.Monic ;)
Polynomial__Monic : (R : Set) -> (p : Elem (Polynomial R)) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:Polynomial.Monic.decidable ;)
Polynomial__Monic__decidable : (R : Set) -> (p : Elem (Polynomial R)) -> Proof (Decidable (Polynomial__Monic R p)).

(; doc: `X` is the polynomial variable (aka indeterminate). ;)
(; //LEAN:Polynomial.X ;)
Polynomial__X : (R : Set) -> Elem (Polynomial R).

(; doc: The composition of polynomials as a polynomial. ;)
(; //LEAN:Polynomial.comp ;)
Polynomial__comp : (R : Set) -> (p : Elem (Polynomial R)) -> (q : Elem (Polynomial R)) -> Elem (Polynomial R).

(; doc: `degree p` is the degree of the polynomial `p`, i.e. the largest `X`-exponent in `p`. `degree p = some n` when `p ≠ 0` and `n` is the highest power of `X` that appears in `p`, otherwise `degree 0 = ⊥`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Polynomial.degree ;)
Polynomial__degree : (R : Set) -> (p : Elem (Polynomial R)) -> Elem (WithBot Nat).

(; //LEAN:Polynomial.degree_modByMonic_lt._unary ;)
Polynomial__degree_modByMonic_lt___unary : (R : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Ring R)) -> (_x : Elem (PSigma (Polynomial R) (p => PSigma (Polynomial R) (q => Polynomial__Monic R q)))) -> Proof (Lt (Polynomial__degree R (Polynomial__modByMonic R (PSigma__proj0 _x) (PSigma__proj0 (PSigma__proj1 _x)))) (Polynomial__degree R (PSigma__proj0 (PSigma__proj1 _x)))).

(; doc: See `divByMonic`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Polynomial.divModByMonicAux._unary ;)
Polynomial__divModByMonicAux___unary : (R : Set) -> (_x : Elem (PSigma (Polynomial R) (_p => PSigma (Polynomial R) (q => Polynomial__Monic R q)))) -> Elem (Prod (Polynomial R) (Polynomial R)).

(; doc: `eval x p` is the evaluation of the polynomial `p` at `x` ;)
(; //LEAN:Polynomial.eval ;)
Polynomial__eval : (R : Set) -> (x1 : Elem R) -> (p : Elem (Polynomial R)) -> Elem R.

(; doc: Evaluate a polynomial `p` given a ring hom `f` from the scalar ring to the target and a value `x` for the variable in the target ;)
(; //LEAN:Polynomial.eval₂ ;)
Polynomial__eval_u2082_ : (R : Set) -> (S : Set) -> (f : Elem (RingHom R S)) -> (x2 : Elem S) -> (p : Elem (Polynomial R)) -> Elem S.

(; //LEAN:Polynomial.instDecidableEq ;)
Polynomial__instDecidableEq : (R : Set) -> Proof (DecidableEq (Polynomial R)).

(; doc: `leadingCoeff p` gives the coefficient of the highest power of `X` in `p`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Polynomial.leadingCoeff ;)
Polynomial__leadingCoeff : (R : Set) -> (p : Elem (Polynomial R)) -> Elem R.

(; //LEAN:Polynomial.leadingCoeff_ne_zero ;)
Polynomial__leadingCoeff_ne_zero : (R : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring R)) -> (p : Elem (Polynomial R)) -> Proof (iff (Neq (Polynomial__leadingCoeff R p) 0) (Neq p 0)).

(; doc: `map f p` maps a polynomial `p` across a ring hom `f` ;)
(; //LEAN:Polynomial.map ;)
Polynomial__map : (R : Set) -> (S : Set) -> (f : Elem (RingHom R S)) -> (x2 : Elem (Polynomial R)) -> Elem (Polynomial S).

(; //LEAN:Polynomial.modByMonic_eq_sub_mul_div._unary ;)
Polynomial__modByMonic_eq_sub_mul_div___unary : (R : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Ring R)) -> (_x : Elem (PSigma (Polynomial R) (p => Polynomial R))) -> Proof (Eqt (Polynomial R) (Polynomial__modByMonic R (PSigma__proj0 _x) (PSigma__proj1 _x)) (minus (PSigma__proj0 _x) (times (PSigma__proj1 _x) (Polynomial__divByMonic R (PSigma__proj0 _x) (PSigma__proj1 _x))))).

(; //LEAN:Polynomial.monic_X_sub_C ;)
Polynomial__monic_X_sub_C : (R : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Ring R)) -> (x1 : Elem R) -> Proof (Polynomial__Monic R (minus (Polynomial__X R) (Polynomial__C__app x1))).

(; //LEAN:Polynomial.monic_one ;)
Polynomial__monic_one : (R : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring R)) -> Proof (Polynomial__Monic R 1).

(; doc: `natDegree p` forces `degree p` to ℕ, by defining `natDegree 0 = 0`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Polynomial.natDegree ;)
Polynomial__natDegree : (R : Set) -> (p : Elem (Polynomial R)) -> Elem Nat.

(; //LEAN:Polynomial.natDegree_eq_of_degree_eq_some ;)
Polynomial__natDegree_eq_of_degree_eq_some : (R : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring R)) -> (p : Elem (Polynomial R)) -> (n : Elem Nat) -> (h : Proof (Eqt (WithBot Nat) (Polynomial__degree R p) (Nat__cast (WithBot Nat) n))) -> Proof (Eq (Polynomial__natDegree R p) n).

(; //LEAN:Polynomial.natDegree_le_natDegree ;)
Polynomial__natDegree_le_natDegree : (R : Set) -> (S : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring R)) -> (p : Elem (Polynomial R)) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring S)) -> (q : Elem (Polynomial S)) -> (hpq : Proof (Leq (Polynomial__degree R p) (Polynomial__degree S q))) -> Proof (Leq (Polynomial__natDegree R p) (Polynomial__natDegree S q)).

(; doc: `natTrailingDegree p` forces `trailingDegree p` to `ℕ`, by defining `natTrailingDegree ⊤ = 0`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Polynomial.natTrailingDegree ;)
Polynomial__natTrailingDegree : (R : Set) -> (p : Elem (Polynomial R)) -> Elem Nat.

(; //LEAN:Polynomial.subsingleton_isRoot_of_natDegree_eq_one ;)
Polynomial__subsingleton_isRoot_of_natDegree_eq_one : (R : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring R)) -> (p : Elem (Polynomial R)) -> (_ : Proof (IsLeftCancelMulZero R)) -> (h1 : Proof (Eq (Polynomial__natDegree R p) 1)) -> Proof (Set__Subsingleton R (Set__ofPred R (x1 => Polynomial__IsRoot R p x1))).

(; doc: `trailingCoeff p` gives the coefficient of the smallest power of `X` in `p`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Polynomial.trailingCoeff ;)
Polynomial__trailingCoeff : (R : Set) -> (p : Elem (Polynomial R)) -> Elem R.

(; doc: The product type, usually written `α × β`. Product types are also called pair or tuple types. Elements of this type are pairs in which the first element is an `α` and the second element is a `β`.  Products nest to the right, so `(x, y, z) : α × β × γ` is equivalent to `(x, (y, z)) : α × (β × γ)`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `×` in identifiers is `Prod`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Prod ;)
Prod : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: The first element of a pair. ;)
(; //LEAN:Prod.fst ;)
Prod__fst : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (self : Elem (Prod X Y)) -> Elem X.

(; doc: The second element of a pair. ;)
(; //LEAN:Prod.snd ;)
Prod__snd : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (self : Elem (Prod X Y)) -> Elem Y.

(; doc: A `Ring` is a `Semiring` with negation making it an additive group. ;)
(; //LEAN:Ring ;)
Ring : (R : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Bundled semiring homomorphisms; use this for bundled ring homomorphisms too.  This extends from both `MonoidHom` and `MonoidWithZeroHom` in order to put the fields in a sensible order, even though `MonoidWithZeroHom` already extends `MonoidHom`. ;)
(; //LEAN:RingHom ;)
RingHom : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: A `Semiring` is a type with addition, multiplication, a `0` and a `1` where addition is commutative and associative, multiplication is associative and left and right distributive over addition, and `0` and `1` are additive and multiplicative identities. ;)
(; //LEAN:Semiring ;)
Semiring : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A set `s` is a `Subsingleton` if it has at most one element. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.Subsingleton ;)
Set__Subsingleton : (X : Set) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Prop.

(; doc: Turn a predicate `p : α → Prop` into a set, also written as `{x | p x}` ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.ofPred ;)
Set__ofPred : (X : Set) -> (Mem : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> Elem (powerset X).

(; doc: `True` is a proposition and has only an introduction rule, `True.intro : True`. In other words, `True` is simply true, and has a canonical proof, `True.intro` For more information: [Propositional Logic](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/propositions_and_proofs.html#propositional-logic) ;)
(; //LEAN:True ;)
True : Prop.

(; doc: Attach `⊥` to a type. ;)
(; //LEAN:WithBot ;)
WithBot : (X : Set) -> Set.

(; //LEAN:and_iff_right ;)
and_iff_right : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (ha : Proof a) -> Proof (iff (and a b) b).

(; doc: Congruence in both function and argument. If `f₁ = f₂` and `a₁ = a₂` then `f₁ a₁ = f₂ a₂`. This only works for nondependent functions; the theorem statement is more complex in the dependent case.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:congr ;)
congr : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f1 : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (f2 : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (a1 : Elem X) -> (a2 : Elem X) -> (h1 : Proof (Eqt (Elem X -> Y) f1 f2)) -> (h2 : Proof (Eqt X a1 a2)) -> Proof (Eqt Y (f1 a1) (f2 a2)).

(; doc: Congruence in the function argument: if `a₁ = a₂` then `f a₁ = f a₂` for any (nondependent) function `f`. This is more powerful than it might look at first, because you can also use a lambda expression for `f` to prove that `<something containing a₁> = <something containing a₂>`. This function is used internally by tactics like `congr` and `simp` to apply equalities inside subterms.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:congrArg ;)
congrArg : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (a1 : Elem X) -> (a2 : Elem X) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X a1 a2)) -> Proof (Eqt Y (f a1) (f a2)).

(; doc: "Dependent" if-then-else, normally written via the notation `if h : c then t(h) else e(h)`, is sugar for `dite c (fun h => t(h)) (fun h => e(h))`, and it is the same as `if c then t else e` except that `t` is allowed to depend on a proof `h : c`, and `e` can depend on `h : ¬c`. (Both branches use the same name for the hypothesis, even though it has different types in the two cases.)  We use this to be able to communicate the if-then-else condition to the branches. For example, `Array.get arr i h` expects a proof `h : i < arr.size` in order to avoid a bounds check, so you can write `if h : i < arr.size then arr.get i h else ...` to avoid the bounds check inside the if branch. (Of course in this case we have only lifted the check into an explicit `if`, but we could also use this proof multiple times or derive `i < arr.size` from some other proposition that we are checking in the `if`.)  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `if h : c then t else e` in identifiers is `dite` (use `left` for `t` and `right` for `e`). ;)
(; //LEAN:dite ;)
dite : (X : Set) -> (c : Prop) -> (t : (Proof c -> X)) -> (e : (Proof (not c) -> X)) -> Elem X.

(; //LEAN:dite_eq_left ;)
dite_eq_left : (c : Prop) -> (h : Proof (Decidable c)) -> (hc : Proof c) -> (X : Set) -> (t : (Proof c -> X)) -> (e : (Proof (not c) -> X)) -> Proof (Eqt X (dite X c t e) (t hc)).

(; //LEAN:dite_eq_right ;)
dite_eq_right : (c : Prop) -> (h : Proof (Decidable c)) -> (hnc : Proof (not c)) -> (X : Set) -> (t : (Proof c -> X)) -> (e : (Proof (not c) -> X)) -> Proof (Eqt X (dite X c t e) (e hnc)).

(; //LEAN:eq_sub_iff_add_eq ;)
eq_sub_iff_add_eq : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddGroup G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> (c : Elem G) -> Proof (iff (Eqt G a (minus b c)) (Eqt G (plus a c) b)).

(; doc: The identity function. `id` takes an implicit argument `α : Sort u` (a type in any universe), and an argument `a : α`, and returns `a`.  Although this may look like a useless function, one application of the identity function is to explicitly put a type on an expression. If `e` has type `T`, and `T'` is definitionally equal to `T`, then `@id T' e` typechecks, and Lean knows that this expression has type `T'` rather than `T`. This can make a difference for typeclass inference, since `T` and `T'` may have different typeclass instances on them. `show T' from e` is sugar for an `@id T' e` expression. ;)
(; //LEAN:id ;)
id : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; //LEAN:isUnit_iff_ne_zero ;)
isUnit_iff_ne_zero : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (GroupWithZero X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (iff (IsUnit X a) (Neq a 0)).

(; //LEAN:isUnit_one._simp_2 ;)
isUnit_one___simp_2 : (M : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Monoid M)) -> Proof (Eqt Prop (IsUnit M 1) True).

(; doc: `if c then t else e` is notation for `ite c t e`, "if-then-else", which decides to return `t` or `e` depending on whether `c` is true or false. The explicit argument `c : Prop` does not have any actual computational content, but there is an additional `[Decidable c]` argument synthesized by typeclass inference which actually determines how to evaluate `c` to true or false. Write `if h : c then t else e` instead for a "dependent if-then-else" `dite`, which allows `t`/`e` to use the fact that `c` is true/false.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `if c then t else e` in identifiers is `ite` (use `left` for `t` and `right` for `e`). ;)
(; //LEAN:ite ;)
ite : (X : Set) -> (c : Prop) -> (t : Elem X) -> (e : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; //LEAN:ite_eq_right ;)
ite_eq_right : (c : Prop) -> (h : Proof (Decidable c)) -> (hnc : Proof (not c)) -> (X : Set) -> (t : Elem X) -> (e : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X (ite X c t e) e).

(; doc: A `ℕ`-valued version of `emultiplicity`, returning `0` instead of `⊤`. ;)
(; //LEAN:multiplicity ;)
multiplicity : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> Elem Nat.

(; //LEAN:of_eq_true ;)
of_eq_true : (p : Prop) -> (h : Proof (Eqt Prop p True)) -> Proof p.

(; //LEAN:one_dvd ;)
one_dvd : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Monoid X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (divide 1 a).

(; //LEAN:pow_multiplicity_dvd ;)
pow_multiplicity_dvd : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Monoid X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> Proof (divide (pow a (multiplicity X a b)) b).

(; //LEAN:pow_zero ;)
pow_zero : (M : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Monoid M)) -> (a : Elem M) -> Proof (Eqt M (pow a 0) 1).

(; doc: The [axiom](https://lean-lang.org/doc/reference/4.36.0-rc1/find/?domain=Verso.Genre.Manual.section&name=axioms) of **propositional extensionality**. It asserts that if propositions `a` and `b` are logically equivalent (that is, if `a` can be proved from `b` and vice versa), then `a` and `b` are *equal*, meaning `a` can be replaced with `b` in all contexts.  The standard logical connectives provably respect propositional extensionality. However, an axiom is needed for higher order expressions like `P a` where `P : Prop → Prop` is unknown, as well as for equality. Propositional extensionality is intuitionistically valid. ;)
(; //LEAN:propext ;)
propext : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (h : Proof (iff a b)) -> Proof (Eqt Prop a b).

(; doc: `rfl : a = a` is the unique constructor of the equality type. This is the same as `Eq.refl` except that it takes `a` implicitly instead of explicitly.  This is a more powerful theorem than it may appear at first, because although the statement of the theorem is `a = a`, Lean will allow anything that is definitionally equal to that type. So, for instance, `2 + 2 = 4` is proven in Lean by `rfl`, because both sides are the same up to definitional equality. ;)
(; //LEAN:rfl ;)
rfl : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X a a).

